---A single rule, which you can use in a WebACL or RuleGroup to identify web requests that you want to manage in some way. Each rule includes one top-level Statement that WAF uses to identify matching web requests, and parameters that govern how WAF handles them.
---@class aws.wafv2.Rule
---@field Name aws.wafv2.EntityName The name of the rule.
---@field Priority aws.wafv2.RulePriority If you define more than one Rule in a WebACL, WAF evaluates each request against the Rules in order based on the value of Priority. WAF processes rules with lower priority first. The priorities don't need to be consecutive, but they must all be different.
---@field Statement aws.wafv2.Statement The WAF processing statement for the rule, for example ByteMatchStatement or SizeConstraintStatement.
---@field Action? aws.wafv2.RuleAction The action that WAF should take on a web request when it matches the rule statement. Settings at the web ACL level can override the rule action setting.
---@field OverrideAction? aws.wafv2.OverrideAction The action to use in the place of the action that results from the rule group evaluation. Set the override action to none to leave the result of the rule group alone. Set it to count to override the result to count only.
---@field RuleLabels? aws.wafv2.Labels Labels to apply to web requests that match the rule match statement. WAF applies fully qualified labels to matching web requests. A fully qualified label is the concatenation of a label namespace and a rule label. The rule's rule group or web ACL defines the label namespace.
---@field VisibilityConfig aws.wafv2.VisibilityConfig Defines and enables Amazon CloudWatch metrics and web request sample collection.
---@field CaptchaConfig? aws.wafv2.CaptchaConfig Specifies how WAF should handle CAPTCHA evaluations. If you don't specify this, WAF uses the CAPTCHA configuration that's defined for the web ACL.
---@field ChallengeConfig? aws.wafv2.ChallengeConfig Specifies how WAF should handle Challenge evaluations. If you don't specify this, WAF uses the challenge configuration that's defined for the web ACL.

---The action that WAF should take on a web request when it matches a rule's statement. Settings at the web ACL level can override the rule action setting.
---@class aws.wafv2.RuleAction
---@field Block? aws.wafv2.BlockAction Instructs WAF to block the web request.
---@field Allow? aws.wafv2.AllowAction Instructs WAF to allow the web request.
---@field Count? aws.wafv2.CountAction Instructs WAF to count the web request and then continue evaluating the request using the remaining rules in the web ACL.
---@field Captcha? aws.wafv2.CaptchaAction Instructs WAF to run a CAPTCHA check against the web request.
---@field Challenge? aws.wafv2.ChallengeAction Instructs WAF to run a Challenge check against the web request.
---@field Monetize? aws.wafv2.MonetizeAction Instructs WAF to return an HTTP 402 Payment Required response with a price manifest. The requesting client can complete payment and resubmit the request to gain access. This is a terminating action-requests that do not complete payment are blocked. This action is available only for web ACLs associated with Amazon CloudFront distributions and requires a MonetizationConfig on the web ACL.

---Action setting to use in the place of a rule action that is configured inside the rule group. You specify one override for each rule whose action you want to change.
---@class aws.wafv2.RuleActionOverride
---@field Name aws.wafv2.EntityName The name of the rule to override.
---@field ActionToUse aws.wafv2.RuleAction The override action to use, in place of the configured action of the rule in the rule group.

---@alias aws.wafv2.RuleActionOverrides aws.wafv2.RuleActionOverride[]

---A rule group defines a collection of rules to inspect and control web requests that you can use in a WebACL. When you create a rule group, you define an immutable capacity limit. If you update a rule group, you must stay within the capacity. This allows others to reuse the rule group with confidence in its capacity requirements.
